1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,577
On the left: Manim
Community, the real thing,

2
00:00:02,577 --> 00:00:03,947
installed on a computer.

3
00:00:03,947 --> 00:00:06,711
On the right: ManimOnline,
in a browser tab.

4
00:00:06,711 --> 00:00:08,545
Same code, frame for frame.

5
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:12,283
Including this. The square that collapses
to a point when you rotate it with

6
00:00:12,283 --> 00:00:14,559
animate. We met it in episode three,

7
00:00:14,559 --> 00:00:16,544
and it's arguably a surprising behaviour.

8
00:00:16,544 --> 00:00:19,540
But it's Manim's behaviour,
and so ManimOnline does it too,

9
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:20,445
deliberately.

10
00:00:20,445 --> 00:00:23,742
Last episode, we saw that the real
Manim can't run in the browser,

11
00:00:23,742 --> 00:00:25,937
so ManimOnline brings its own engine.

12
00:00:25,937 --> 00:00:27,678
This episode is about that engine,

13
00:00:27,678 --> 00:00:29,849
and about a harder question
than how to build it:

14
00:00:29,849 --> 00:00:32,000
how do you know it's right?

15
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,241
Manim stands on a lot of other software.

16
00:00:34,241 --> 00:00:36,795
It draws with a graphics
library called Cairo,

17
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:39,059
lays out text with another called Pango,

18
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:41,114
encodes video with FFmpeg,

19
00:00:41,114 --> 00:00:44,725
and calls out to a separately
installed LaTeX for formulas.

20
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:47,418
Several of those are
large compiled libraries,

21
00:00:47,418 --> 00:00:51,436
and running them inside a web page is
either impossible today or would mean an

22
00:00:51,436 --> 00:00:52,620
enormous download.

23
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:54,954
So ManimOnline took a different approach.

24
00:00:54,954 --> 00:00:57,171
Keep the parts people actually type:

25
00:00:57,171 --> 00:00:58,901
the class names, the method names,

26
00:00:58,901 --> 00:01:00,573
their arguments and their results.

27
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:02,709
And reimplement everything underneath,

28
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:06,297
in plain Python that runs
comfortably in Pyodide.

29
00:01:06,297 --> 00:01:10,105
Instead of pixels, it produces the
frame snapshots we saw last episode,

30
00:01:10,105 --> 00:01:11,324
and the page draws them.

31
00:01:11,324 --> 00:01:13,217
That engine is called manim-lite.

32
00:01:13,217 --> 00:01:16,874
It's a single Python file, about
twenty eight thousand lines long.

33
00:01:16,874 --> 00:01:19,579
And its goal is simple to
state and hard to achieve:

34
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:20,891
for everything it supports,

35
00:01:20,891 --> 00:01:25,217
the same code should produce the
same animation as Manim Community.

36
00:01:25,217 --> 00:01:27,945
What does "the same" actually mean?

37
00:01:27,945 --> 00:01:30,309
Not that the code looks similar inside.

38
00:01:30,309 --> 00:01:33,216
manim-lite is written
completely differently.

39
00:01:33,216 --> 00:01:36,328
It means that everything
you can observe agrees:

40
00:01:36,328 --> 00:01:39,543
the shapes and their
exact sizes and positions,

41
00:01:39,543 --> 00:01:42,237
the colours, how long
each animation takes,

42
00:01:42,237 --> 00:01:45,464
how it moves, and which
objects exist at each moment.

43
00:01:45,464 --> 00:01:48,181
That definition has an
important consequence.

44
00:01:48,181 --> 00:01:50,282
You can't confirm it by reading code,

45
00:01:50,282 --> 00:01:51,328
however carefully.

46
00:01:51,328 --> 00:01:55,972
Manim is a large library with years'
worth of accumulated decisions in its

47
00:01:55,972 --> 00:02:00,140
design, and many of them only show
up when you actually run something.

48
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:03,809
The only way to know whether two
engines agree is to run both,

49
00:02:03,809 --> 00:02:05,767
and compare.

50
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:07,276
So that's what happens.

51
00:02:07,276 --> 00:02:09,447
First, a set of reference snippets:

52
00:02:09,447 --> 00:02:13,279
small pieces of code chosen
to pin down tricky geometry,

53
00:02:13,279 --> 00:02:18,097
layout and sizes. Their results were
measured once with Manim Community,

54
00:02:18,097 --> 00:02:20,035
version zero point twenty two,

55
00:02:20,035 --> 00:02:22,497
and saved. Whenever manim-lite changes,

56
00:02:22,497 --> 00:02:26,398
its answers are checked against
those measurements automatically.

57
00:02:26,398 --> 00:02:30,136
If a change moves a shape by
even a tiny fraction of a unit,

58
00:02:30,136 --> 00:02:31,367
the check fails.

59
00:02:31,367 --> 00:02:33,091
Second, and much bigger:

60
00:02:33,091 --> 00:02:36,046
Manim's documentation is
full of example scenes,

61
00:02:36,046 --> 00:02:38,310
each written to show off some feature.

62
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:42,210
Every one of those examples is
extracted and run through both engines,

63
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:45,879
and the final positions, sizes
and durations are compared.

64
00:02:45,879 --> 00:02:47,992
Not every difference is a bug.

65
00:02:47,992 --> 00:02:52,183
Text, for example, is measured using
the fonts installed on the machine,

66
00:02:52,183 --> 00:02:57,245
so a laptop and a Linux server can
disagree slightly about how wide a word is,

67
00:02:57,245 --> 00:02:59,277
even with the real Manim.

68
00:02:59,277 --> 00:03:03,967
Part of the work is sorting differences
into "manim-lite is wrong" and "this

69
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:08,833
comparison isn't fair", and only
trusting the comparisons that are.

70
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:11,794
Documentation examples
are written to work.

71
00:03:11,794 --> 00:03:15,729
Bugs often hide in combinations
nobody thought to write down.

72
00:03:15,729 --> 00:03:17,030
So there are fuzzers:

73
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:21,140
programs that generate random
chains of methods and random scenes,

74
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:24,507
with several animations
and updaters overlapping,

75
00:03:24,507 --> 00:03:27,189
and compare both engines on each one.

76
00:03:27,189 --> 00:03:28,918
Whenever a difference turns up,

77
00:03:28,918 --> 00:03:32,471
it's investigated, fixed, and
turned into a permanent test,

78
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:34,816
so it can never quietly come back.

79
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:38,624
And Manim Community has its
own suite of unit tests.

80
00:03:38,624 --> 00:03:41,318
Those can be pointed at manim-lite too.

81
00:03:41,318 --> 00:03:44,301
The tests that still fail
fall into known categories,

82
00:03:44,301 --> 00:03:47,123
like tests that need real LaTeX files,

83
00:03:47,123 --> 00:03:49,968
or that check how video
files are written to disk,

84
00:03:49,968 --> 00:03:51,802
which don't apply in a browser.

85
00:03:51,802 --> 00:03:55,598
All of this sits on top of
ManimOnline's own tests:

86
00:03:55,598 --> 00:03:56,492
hundreds of them,

87
00:03:56,492 --> 00:04:00,463
plus checks that open the real editor
in three different browser engines and

88
00:04:00,463 --> 00:04:04,650
confirm that scenes
actually render and play.

89
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:09,085
Let's look at a few cases where matching
Manim meant reproducing something you

90
00:04:09,085 --> 00:04:10,292
might not expect.

91
00:04:10,292 --> 00:04:12,057
The collapsing square.

92
00:04:12,057 --> 00:04:14,971
This scene draws the path of every corner.

93
00:04:14,971 --> 00:04:19,325
With animate, each corner travels in a
straight line to the opposite corner,

94
00:04:19,325 --> 00:04:21,229
and they all meet in the middle.

95
00:04:21,229 --> 00:04:24,397
With Rotate, they travel
around semicircles.

96
00:04:24,397 --> 00:04:29,380
manim-lite actually stores
shapes differently from Manim.

97
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,678
It often keeps a shape's position,

98
00:04:31,678 --> 00:04:35,150
scale and rotation
separately from its outline,

99
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:37,402
which makes many operations cheaper.

100
00:04:37,402 --> 00:04:42,893
But a naive blend of "rotation angle
from start to end" would spin the

101
00:04:42,893 --> 00:04:46,783
square rigidly, which
is not what Manim does.

102
00:04:46,783 --> 00:04:50,510
So manim-lite blends the
pose in a specific way,

103
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:55,293
treating scale and rotation
together as a single complex number,

104
00:04:55,293 --> 00:05:00,657
which gives exactly the same straight-line
paths as Manim's point-by-point

105
00:05:00,657 --> 00:05:07,433
method. Correct, here, means
faithfully reproducing the collapse.

106
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:09,395
Here's another. Scale an arrow down,

107
00:05:09,395 --> 00:05:11,624
and its tip does not shrink with it.

108
00:05:11,624 --> 00:05:15,177
Compare the home-made arrow, a line
and a triangle grouped together,

109
00:05:15,177 --> 00:05:17,185
whose head shrinks in proportion.

110
00:05:17,185 --> 00:05:20,471
That's because Manim's arrow does
something particular when scaled:

111
00:05:20,471 --> 00:05:22,584
it takes its tip off, scales the shaft,

112
00:05:22,584 --> 00:05:23,884
and attaches the tip again.

113
00:05:23,884 --> 00:05:27,542
So the tip keeps its size, up to a
limit tied to the arrow's length,

114
00:05:27,542 --> 00:05:30,478
which is why very short
arrows still get smaller tips.

115
00:05:30,478 --> 00:05:32,046
It sounds like a small detail.

116
00:05:32,046 --> 00:05:33,845
But it changes where the tip ends up,

117
00:05:33,845 --> 00:05:37,897
how long the shaft is, and what happens
in every later animation of that arrow.

118
00:05:37,897 --> 00:05:40,231
manim-lite mirrors Manim's remove,

119
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:42,657
scale, re-attach sequence literally,

120
00:05:42,657 --> 00:05:46,583
because subtly different would
accumulate into visibly different.

121
00:05:46,583 --> 00:05:48,696
Here's one that surprises most people.

122
00:05:48,696 --> 00:05:51,378
Write the same word
with Text and with Tex,

123
00:05:51,378 --> 00:05:53,503
both at font size ninety six.

124
00:05:53,503 --> 00:05:55,186
They come out different sizes.

125
00:05:55,186 --> 00:05:57,485
The Text version is about a third larger.

126
00:05:57,485 --> 00:06:01,711
In Manim, those two kinds of text go
through completely different software,

127
00:06:01,711 --> 00:06:05,740
and each converts font size into
screen units with its own rule.

128
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:10,186
For Text, the size of a letter's em
square is the font size divided by seventy

129
00:06:10,186 --> 00:06:14,076
two. For Tex, it's the font
size divided by ninety six.

130
00:06:14,076 --> 00:06:15,539
Those numbers weren't guessed.

131
00:06:15,539 --> 00:06:17,733
They were measured from Manim itself.

132
00:06:17,733 --> 00:06:20,926
An earlier version of
manim-lite used a single,

133
00:06:20,926 --> 00:06:25,024
made-up scale for both, and its
text was simply the wrong size.

134
00:06:25,024 --> 00:06:27,055
A plausible guess, it turns out,

135
00:06:27,055 --> 00:06:29,783
is not the same as a measurement.

136
00:06:29,783 --> 00:06:31,873
Some features in Manim are random.

137
00:06:31,873 --> 00:06:34,891
Stream lines start from points
with a little random jitter,

138
00:06:34,891 --> 00:06:36,517
so they don't look mechanical.

139
00:06:36,517 --> 00:06:40,360
Graph layouts place vertices by
simulating springs from random starting

140
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:41,265
positions.

141
00:06:41,265 --> 00:06:43,820
But computer randomness is reproducible:

142
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:46,385
a generator starts from
a number called a seed,

143
00:06:46,385 --> 00:06:48,808
and produces the same sequence every time.

144
00:06:48,808 --> 00:06:51,958
Manim uses NumPy's generator
for the stream lines,

145
00:06:51,958 --> 00:06:55,546
and the networkx library's
random choices for graph layouts.

146
00:06:55,546 --> 00:06:59,122
So for manim-lite to draw the same
stream lines and the same graphs,

147
00:06:59,122 --> 00:07:01,397
it has to produce the same random numbers,

148
00:07:01,397 --> 00:07:03,255
bit for bit. And it does:

149
00:07:03,255 --> 00:07:06,575
it contains exact reimplementations
of those generators,

150
00:07:06,575 --> 00:07:09,837
an algorithm called P C G sixty four,

151
00:07:09,837 --> 00:07:11,973
and another called the Mersenne Twister.

152
00:07:11,973 --> 00:07:14,574
Same seed, same numbers, same picture,

153
00:07:14,574 --> 00:07:17,666
whether or not NumPy is even loaded.

154
00:07:17,666 --> 00:07:20,336
One last case, and it's the most extreme.

155
00:07:20,336 --> 00:07:21,950
Here are the same three numbers,

156
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:24,005
added in two different orders.

157
00:07:24,005 --> 00:07:27,070
The answers differ, in the
sixteenth decimal place.

158
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:29,613
Computers store numbers
with finite precision,

159
00:07:29,613 --> 00:07:31,598
so rounding happens at every step,

160
00:07:31,598 --> 00:07:33,526
and the order of the steps matters.

161
00:07:33,526 --> 00:07:34,954
Normally that's irrelevant.

162
00:07:34,954 --> 00:07:36,567
But some of Manim's searches,

163
00:07:36,567 --> 00:07:39,458
like finding where along a
curve a given point lies,

164
00:07:39,458 --> 00:07:43,522
can land on a near tie, where the
sixteenth decimal place decides the answer.

165
00:07:43,522 --> 00:07:47,017
So in a few places manim-lite
reproduces not just Manim's formulas,

166
00:07:47,017 --> 00:07:49,525
but the exact order in
which NumPy adds things up.

167
00:07:49,525 --> 00:07:52,683
That's how far "the same"
sometimes has to go.

168
00:07:52,683 --> 00:07:55,417
All of this effort deserves
an honest boundary.

169
00:07:55,417 --> 00:07:57,722
manim-lite is a compatibility subset,

170
00:07:57,722 --> 00:07:59,359
not Manim Community.

171
00:07:59,359 --> 00:08:01,135
It doesn't have real LaTeX:

172
00:08:01,135 --> 00:08:03,167
formulas go through MathJax.

173
00:08:03,167 --> 00:08:05,373
Text uses built-in font measurements,

174
00:08:05,373 --> 00:08:07,184
not your computer's fonts.

175
00:08:07,184 --> 00:08:09,761
Previews run at fifteen frames per second.

176
00:08:09,761 --> 00:08:12,060
And there are features it
doesn't support at all.

177
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:15,160
The design principle is that
when something isn't supported,

178
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:17,122
it should say so with a clear error,

179
00:08:17,122 --> 00:08:19,780
not quietly draw something different.

180
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:23,147
A wrong picture that looks plausible
is far worse than an error message,

181
00:08:23,147 --> 00:08:25,700
because you might never notice.

182
00:08:25,700 --> 00:08:27,848
The real Manim can't run in the browser,

183
00:08:27,848 --> 00:08:31,865
so manim-lite reimplements
its interface on a new engine.

184
00:08:31,865 --> 00:08:34,849
"The same" means the
same observable result,

185
00:08:34,849 --> 00:08:37,008
and the only way to know is to measure:

186
00:08:37,008 --> 00:08:40,955
against recorded references,
against every documentation example,

187
00:08:40,955 --> 00:08:44,647
against random scenes, and
against Manim's own tests.

188
00:08:44,647 --> 00:08:48,618
Along the way, faithfulness means
copying surprising behaviours:

189
00:08:48,618 --> 00:08:51,904
straight paths, fixed
tips, two font scales,

190
00:08:51,904 --> 00:08:55,468
exact random numbers, even
the order of addition.

191
00:08:55,468 --> 00:08:56,745
One question remains.

192
00:08:56,745 --> 00:09:00,692
Every QR code in this series
opened code in this engine,

193
00:09:00,692 --> 00:09:03,107
with no server storing your work.

194
00:09:03,107 --> 00:09:05,696
How does a link, or a
little square of dots,

195
00:09:05,696 --> 00:09:07,658
carry an entire program?

196
00:09:07,658 --> 00:09:09,167
That's our final episode.
